@@ PE1 | Predictive
On a defence project, some team members are not cleared to know the full mission context, and they struggle to see why certain requirements matter. What should the project manager do?
A) Share as much of the purpose as security rules allow, through an unclassified summary
B) Ask the uncleared team members to follow the requirements without asking questions, since security comes first
C) Share the full mission context with everyone informally, since a common vision matters more than secrecy
D) Replace the uncleared team members with cleared staff, even if they lack the necessary technical skills
= A
? A common vision can be built within security constraints. An approved, unclassified description of goals and user needs gives everyone the purpose they need without breaching need-to-know rules.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid
A railway upgrade involves engineers, train operators and station staff, who each describe the end result in their own terms. Which technique would best help them picture the same end state?
A) A future-state scenario told from a passenger's point of view
B) A detailed technical specification of the signalling system
C) A list of each group's objectives, kept in separate documents for clarity
D) A Gantt chart showing the main construction milestones
= A
? Describing the future state through the eyes of the customer gives diverse groups a shared, concrete picture of what success looks like.

@@ PE1 | Agile
The developers of a farming app increasingly focus on technical elegance, and recent features ignore what farmers actually need. What should the product owner do?
A) Reconnect the team with users, for example by inviting farmers to reviews or visiting farms together
B) Tell the developers to stop improving the code until the farmers' needs have been met
C) Write more detailed requirements so that the developers have less room for interpretation
D) Ask the sponsor to remind the team of the project's goals at the next all-hands meeting
= A
? Direct contact with users renews the team's understanding of the product's purpose and refocuses the work on customer value.

@@ PE1 | Hybrid | multi
Which two practices help keep a programme's vision alive over several years? (Choose two.)
A) Revisiting the vision at major planning events
B) Linking each increment's goals explicitly to the vision
C) Presenting the vision once, at the kickoff meeting
D) Rewriting the vision at the start of every sprint
E) Keeping the vision confidential to senior managers
= A, B
? A vision stays meaningful when it is revisited regularly and visibly connected to current work. A vision that is shown once, changed constantly or kept secret does not guide the team.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Three teams build parts of one product, each with its own definition of done, and integration often fails. What should be agreed?
A) A shared minimum definition of done for all of the teams
B) Separate integration teams that fix problems after each sprint
C) A rule that each team tests only its own components
D) A longer sprint, so that integration has more time
= A
? A shared definition of done gives all teams the same understanding of quality and completeness, which makes integration predictable.

@@ PE1 | Predictive
A rail authority and its main contractor have a history of adversarial relationships on earlier projects. What can the project manager do at the start of the new project?
A) Hold a joint partnering workshop to agree shared objectives and ways of working
B) Rely on the contract's penalty clauses to make sure that the contractor performs
C) Limit contact with the contractor to formal letters to avoid any misunderstandings
D) Ask the sponsor to replace the contractor with a firm that has no history with the authority
= A
? Partnering workshops build a common vision and agreed behaviours between organisations, which reduces conflict and improves collaboration.

@@ PE1 | Agile
Team members disagree about the purpose of the minimum viable product: some think it is meant to test the market, others think it is meant to impress investors. What should happen?
A) The product owner clarifies with stakeholders what the MVP must teach and how success is measured
B) The team builds features that serve both purposes, so that nobody's view is excluded from the MVP
C) The developers vote on the purpose of the MVP and follow the majority view for the rest of the project
D) The scrum master decides on the purpose, since the disagreement is slowing the team down
= A
? An MVP exists to learn. A clear learning goal and success measure, agreed with stakeholders, aligns the team.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
At a client site, the client's IT staff resent the consultants, whom they see as overpaid outsiders, and they withhold information. What should the engagement manager do?
A) Raise it openly with the client lead and involve the IT staff in the work and decisions
B) Report the IT staff to their managers for failing to cooperate with the consultants
C) Work around the IT staff by collecting the information from other departments that are more willing
D) Ignore the tension, since the consultants will leave once the engagement ends
= A
? Involving the people who feel excluded and addressing the issue openly turns resistance into collaboration. Working around them deepens the conflict.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
A safety inspector from the rail regulator interprets a rule more strictly than the project's engineers do. The disagreement is delaying work. What should the project manager do?
A) Seek a formal clarification from the regulator, with the compliance experts
B) Follow the engineers' interpretation, since they know the technical details best
C) Ask the inspector's manager to replace the inspector with a more flexible colleague
D) Continue the work while the disagreement is unresolved and deal with it afterwards
= A
? Disagreements with a regulator are resolved through formal channels and expert input. Proceeding without clarity creates compliance risk.

@@ PE2 | Predictive
Before negotiating with a supplier, the project manager identifies what the project will do if no agreement is reached. What is this called?
A) The project's BATNA
B) The zone of possible agreement
C) The supplier's reservation price
D) The target price of the negotiation
= A
? The best alternative to a negotiated agreement (BATNA) is what a party will do if no agreement is reached. Knowing it tells a negotiator when to walk away and strengthens their position.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Farmers and the agritech company disagree about who owns the data collected from the farmers' fields. The dispute is threatening the pilot. What should the project manager do?
A) Facilitate a discussion of both sides' interests, with legal guidance, to find an agreement
B) Side with the company, since the company paid for the sensors and the data platform
C) Side with the farmers, since the data comes from their land and their crops
D) Pause the pilot until the government passes a law that settles the question of data ownership
= A
? Focusing on interests, supported by legal guidance, allows a collaborative solution, for example data-sharing terms that protect farmers while allowing the service to work.

@@ PE2 | Hybrid
Two team leads keep arguing about who approves design changes. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify the decision rights in a RACI chart agreed with both leads
B) Let the more senior of the two leads approve all of the design changes
C) Ask both leads to approve every change together, whatever its size
D) Tell the leads to sort out the disagreement between themselves
= A
? Many conflicts come from unclear roles. Agreeing and documenting responsibilities, for example in a RACI chart, removes the source of the conflict.

@@ PE2 | Agile
The client's agile coach promotes Scrum, while the consulting firm's teams use Kanban, and the teams are confused about which to follow. What should the engagement manager do?
A) Agree a tailored approach with the coach, based on the type of work and the teams' context
B) Insist on Kanban, since the consulting firm is accountable for delivering the results
C) Adopt Scrum, since the client is paying for the engagement and has the final word
D) Let each team decide for itself, without any coordination between the two approaches or the coach
= A
? The choice of method depends on context. Collaborating with the client to agree a fit-for-purpose approach resolves the conflict and supports delivery.

@@ PE2 | Agile
Two developers ask the scrum master to decide which of their technical solutions is right. What should the scrum master do?
A) Facilitate a discussion that helps them reach a decision themselves
B) Choose the solution that the scrum master personally prefers
C) Ask the product owner to make the technical decision for them
D) Let them build both solutions in parallel and keep whichever one runs faster
= A
? A scrum master coaches the team to resolve its own conflicts and make technical decisions, which builds self-management.

@@ PE2 | Predictive | multi
Which two situations call for escalating a conflict beyond the team? (Choose two.)
A) It involves harassment or a breach of ethics
B) It threatens project objectives and the team cannot resolve it
C) Two members prefer different code editors
D) It is the first disagreement between two members
E) Two members disagree about where to hold the team lunch
= A, B
? Most conflicts are resolved within the team. Harassment, ethical breaches and conflicts that threaten objectives and cannot be resolved locally require escalation.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
According to Daniel Pink, which three factors drive people's motivation for complex, creative work?
A) Autonomy, mastery and purpose
B) Salary, bonuses and job titles
C) Security, status and recognition
D) Rules, supervision and deadlines
= A
? Pink argues that intrinsic motivators (autonomy, mastery and purpose) drive performance in complex work, while external rewards work mainly for simple tasks.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
Over several months, an experienced project manager helps a junior colleague by sharing her own experience and advice. What is this called?
A) Mentoring
B) Coaching
C) Training
D) Delegating
= A
? Mentoring shares the mentor's experience and advice. Coaching helps people find their own answers through questions; training builds specific skills.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
During a field trial, a test drone crashes, and the team is shaken. No one was hurt. What should the project manager do first?
A) Confirm that everyone is safe, then communicate calmly and start a blameless investigation
B) Find out which operator was responsible, so that disciplinary action can be taken quickly
C) Continue the trial with another drone, so that the schedule is not affected by the crash
D) Cancel all further trials until the manufacturer has confirmed that the drones are safe
= A
? In a crisis, a leader ensures safety, stays calm, communicates clearly and focuses on learning rather than blame.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
A team member's parent has been taken to hospital, and she seems distracted at work. What should the project manager do?
A) Talk with her privately, show empathy and agree flexible arrangements
B) Remind her that project deadlines must be met whatever her situation
C) Reassign all of her tasks without discussing the change with her
D) Wait for her to raise the issue, since it is a private family matter
= A
? Emotional intelligence includes empathy. Offering support and flexibility helps the person and maintains trust and commitment.

@@ PE3 | Hybrid
On a transport project, staff from three contractors work in one integrated team, but the contractors' staff feel like second-class members. What should the project manager do?
A) Build one team by including everyone in decisions, recognition and team events
B) Keep the contractors' staff separate, since they are employed by other firms
C) Ask the contractors to motivate their own staff, since that is their responsibility
D) Offer the contractors' staff bonuses that the client's own employees do not receive
= A
? An integrated team works best when all members are treated as part of one team, whatever their employer.

@@ PE3 | Predictive
Despite clear expectations and coaching, a team member's work remains below the agreed standard. What should the project manager do next?
A) Document the issue and follow the performance process with the functional manager
B) Redo the team member's work personally, so that the project is not affected by the problem
C) Remove the team member from the project immediately, without informing anyone else
D) Lower the quality standard for the team member's work, so that the problem disappears
= A
? When coaching does not resolve a performance problem, the project manager follows the formal process with the functional manager, based on documented facts.

@@ PE3 | Agile
A new team asks its scrum master to run all of the meetings and take all of the notes. What should the scrum master do over time?
A) Gradually hand facilitation to team members to build their self-management
B) Continue to run every meeting, since this is the scrum master's main job
C) Stop attending the meetings, so that the team learns to manage on its own
D) Ask the product owner to take over the facilitation of all team meetings
= A
? A servant leader develops the team's capabilities. Gradually transferring facilitation builds ownership and self-management.

@@ PE3 | Agile
The team needs to learn a new data technology, but there is no budget for external training. What should the scrum master suggest?
A) Low-cost learning, such as pairing, internal sessions and learning time within sprint capacity
B) Asking the team members to learn the technology in their own time outside working hours
C) Postponing all stories that need the technology until a training budget becomes available
D) Hiring a contractor who already knows the technology to do all of the related work
= A
? Teams can build skills through pairing, internal knowledge sharing and planned learning time, which also spreads knowledge across the team.

@@ PE3 | Agile | multi
Which two behaviours are typical of servant leadership? (Choose two.)
A) Removing impediments for the team
B) Supporting team members' growth
C) Making all decisions for the team
D) Assigning every task personally
E) Putting personal visibility first
= A, B
? Servant leaders serve the team: they remove obstacles, support growth and enable self-management rather than directing and controlling.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
On a defence project, the customer is a procurement agency, while the end users are soldiers who will use the system in the field. How should the project engage the end users?
A) Through channels agreed with the customer, such as user trials
B) Directly and informally, without involving the procurement agency at all
C) Not at all, since the contract is with the procurement agency only
D) Only after delivery, when the soldiers start using the system
= A
? End users are key stakeholders. On contracted projects, they are engaged through channels agreed with the customer, such as structured trials.

@@ PE4 | Agile
Farmers are the product's key users, but they are hard to reach during the harvest season. How should the team engage them?
A) Plan engagement around the farming calendar, with short visits or calls
B) Postpone all engagement with farmers until the harvest season is over, even if work must wait
C) Rely on the sales team's opinions about what farmers need during the harvest season
D) Send long online surveys that farmers can complete at night after working in the fields
= A
? Engagement is tailored to stakeholders' availability and preferences. Respecting their calendar and keeping interactions short increases participation.

@@ PE4 | Predictive
Which information is typically recorded in the stakeholder register?
A) Identification, assessment and classification
B) The detailed schedule of every meeting held with each stakeholder
C) The stakeholders' personal performance reviews
D) The budget allocated to each stakeholder group
= A
? The stakeholder register records who the stakeholders are, their roles, interests, influence and expectations, and how they are classified.

@@ PE4 | Predictive | match
Match each direction of influence with the stakeholders it refers to.
1) Upward
2) Downward
3) Outward
4) Sideward
A) Senior management, the sponsor and the steering committee
B) The team and the specialists who contribute to the work
C) Groups outside the project, such as suppliers, regulators and the public
D) Peers, such as other project managers competing for resources
= 1-A, 2-B, 3-C, 4-D
? Classifying stakeholders by direction of influence helps the project manager plan how to engage each group.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
A landowner refuses to allow surveyors onto his land for the planned railway route. What should the project manager do first?
A) Meet the landowner to understand his concerns and explore what would make access acceptable
B) Ask the authorities to enforce access to the land immediately, using the project's legal powers
C) Change the railway route to avoid the land, whatever the cost and delay to the project
D) Send the surveyors onto the land anyway, since the survey does not cause any damage
= A
? Understanding a resistant stakeholder's concerns is the first step. Legal measures may be a last resort, but negotiation usually works better and preserves relationships.

@@ PE4 | Agile
The team wants to test five design ideas with real users within a week before committing to build any of them. Which approach fits best?
A) A design sprint with users and stakeholders
B) A detailed business case for each of the ideas
C) A vote among the developers on the best idea
D) A survey of senior managers' preferences
= A
? A design sprint brings users and stakeholders together to prototype and test ideas quickly, so that decisions are based on real feedback.

@@ PE4 | Predictive | multi
Besides power, which two attributes does the salience model use to classify stakeholders? (Choose two.)
A) Legitimacy
B) Urgency
C) Interest
D) Attitude
E) Location
= A, B
? The salience model classifies stakeholders by power, legitimacy and urgency. Stakeholders with all three are "definitive" stakeholders.

@@ PE4 | Hybrid
The consultants' recommendations will mostly affect the client's middle managers, who have not yet been engaged. What should the engagement manager do?
A) Engage them early through interviews and workshops, so their input shapes the work
B) Present the recommendations to them once the board has approved them, to avoid long discussions
C) Ask the board to tell the middle managers to accept whatever the consultants recommend
D) Engage only the managers who are already known to support the consulting engagement
= A
? Engaging the people most affected early improves the quality of the recommendations and their acceptance.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The defence customer asks for a feature that would conflict with the system's security accreditation requirements. What should the project manager do?
A) Explain the constraint and work with the customer on a compliant alternative
B) Build the feature as requested, since the customer is paying and has the final word
C) Refuse the request without discussing the reasons behind it
D) Build the feature but hide it until accreditation is complete
= A
? Aligning expectations means making constraints visible and finding solutions within them. Bypassing accreditation would be a compliance breach.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
The client expects the consultants to implement their recommendations, but the statement of work covers only the analysis. What should the engagement manager do?
A) Clarify the agreed scope and offer to handle implementation as a change
B) Implement the recommendations anyway, so that the client remains satisfied with the engagement
C) Tell the client that implementation will never be possible
D) Ignore the expectation until the analysis has been delivered
= A
? Expectations are aligned against the agreed scope. Additional work can be offered and handled through the change process.

@@ PE5 | Predictive
Users expect the support team to fix any problem within one hour, around the clock, but the support contract specifies the next business day. What should the project manager do?
A) Clarify the agreed service levels and handle any change formally
B) Ask the support team to respond within one hour anyway, so that users are not disappointed
C) Tell the users that the contract cannot be changed under any circumstances
D) Leave the support team to explain the service levels when the first complaints arrive
= A
? Expectations must match agreed service levels. Misaligned expectations are clarified early, and any change is handled formally.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Farmers testing a beta version of the app think it is the final product and complain about every bug. What should the product owner do?
A) Explain the purpose and limits of the beta and how their feedback will be used
B) Stop the beta until all of the bugs have been fixed by the development team
C) Remove the farmers who complain most often from the beta programme to protect morale
D) Promise that the final product will have no bugs at all
= A
? Clear communication about what a beta is for, and how feedback shapes the product, aligns expectations and keeps testers engaged.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid
Executives expect the consultants' report to recommend the option they already favour. The analysis points to a different option. What should the engagement manager do?
A) Present the evidence-based recommendation honestly and explain the reasons
B) Recommend the executives' option, since they are the paying client
C) Present both options as equal, so that nobody is disappointed by the report
D) Delay the report until the executives change their minds on their own
= A
? Honesty and objectivity are core ethical values. Expectations are managed by explaining the evidence, not by changing the conclusions.

@@ PE5 | Agile
Stakeholders treat every item on the product roadmap as a firm commitment. What should the product owner change?
A) Use a now-next-later roadmap that shows growing uncertainty further out
B) Add exact delivery dates to every item so that the roadmap is clearer
C) Remove the roadmap completely and share only the current sprint backlog with stakeholders
D) Lock the roadmap so that no item can move once it has been published
= A
? A now-next-later roadmap communicates direction while making uncertainty visible, which sets realistic expectations about future items.

@@ PE5 | Hybrid | multi
Which two actions help align expectations with a new client at the start of a consulting engagement? (Choose two.)
A) Agreeing deliverables and success criteria in the statement of work
B) Agreeing how and how often progress will be reviewed
C) Promising results beyond the agreed scope to build trust
D) Avoiding any discussion of constraints and assumptions
E) Starting work before the scope has been agreed
= A, B
? Clear deliverables, success criteria and review arrangements set shared expectations from the start.

@@ PE6 | Agile
A farmer in the pilot programme posts a negative review of the app on social media. What should the product owner do?
A) Contact the farmer, understand the problem, address it and share what changed
B) Ask the farmer to remove the review, since the app is still only in a pilot phase with known bugs
C) Ignore the review, since a single farmer does not represent all users
D) Post a reply explaining that the farmer is using the app incorrectly
= A
? Engaging directly with a dissatisfied user, acting on valid feedback and closing the loop can turn a critic into a supporter.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
A weekend rail closure must be extended by two weeks because of unexpected ground conditions. Commuters are already unhappy. What should the project manager do?
A) Inform commuters early, explaining the reason and the replacement travel options
B) Announce the extension on the Friday before the closure to limit complaints
C) Avoid announcing the extension, since commuters will notice it themselves
D) Ask the train operator to announce it, since it is the operator's service
= A
? Bad news is communicated early and honestly, together with the reasons and the alternatives, so that people can plan.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
In the customer's acceptance test, the defence system falls short of a performance requirement, and the customer threatens penalties. What should the project manager do?
A) Analyse the shortfall transparently and present a recovery plan within the contract terms
B) Argue that the requirement was unrealistic and should never have been in the contract
C) Ask the test team to repeat the test until the system passes, since results can vary between runs
D) Offer the customer a discount immediately, before analysing the cause of the shortfall
= A
? Transparency, analysis and a credible recovery plan within the contractual framework protect trust and the relationship.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
The client lead complains that the consultants shared interim findings with the board before she had seen them. What should the engagement manager do?
A) Apologise and agree a review protocol for future deliverables
B) Explain that the board is senior, so it should see findings first
C) Stop sharing interim findings with anyone until the final report
D) Ask the board to keep the findings confidential from the client lead
= A
? Acknowledging the mistake and agreeing how deliverables will be reviewed in future restores trust and prevents a repeat.

@@ PE6 | Hybrid
The Net Promoter Score among pilot farms has fallen for two months in a row. What should the project manager do?
A) Analyse the feedback, address the main issues and tell users what has changed
B) Stop measuring the Net Promoter Score, since it is only an indicator
C) Offer the pilot farms discounts so that their scores improve quickly
D) Wait for the next season, when satisfaction among farmers usually rises again on its own
= A
? Satisfaction data is useful when it leads to action. Closing the loop ("you said, we did") shows stakeholders that their feedback matters.

@@ PE6 | Predictive
The steering committee was surprised by a 15% cost overrun reported at the end of the quarter, although the trend had been visible for two months. What should the project manager do in future?
A) Report trends and forecasts early, with options, instead of waiting for confirmed variances
B) Report only confirmed variances, since early trends may still change before the quarter ends
